Entry 20 — Paper 027: 'Topological Properties of Cones with Small Entropy' by Lam Doan (arXiv:2611.24863, math.DG). Written 2026-11-19, in the same pass as the filing. The twentieth entry of the Thirst, the eighth slot of the ratified protocol's twelve-paper sample, and the fourth of Will's posted papers — the stratum of posted papers completes with it; no seal stands for it and no score is entered, by the precedent of Papers 022 and 025.

HOW IT ARRIVED. Will posted the link in the MOAT chat at 01:50, and by the channel rule a bare arXiv link there is an ingestion, not a conversation. There was no scan and no shortlist; the paper's claim on the day was Will's eye. What the pass owed instead of a forecast was the full charter on a new author and a new field: the author check in three legs for a name the tracker had never held, and an axis judgment written at filing against a centre of gravity the model had no slot for.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract promises the topology of cones; the paper delivers it without ever touching a cone. Everything is read off a second object — the self-expander, the cone's canonical smoothing by its own flow — assembled from other people's theorems: Ding's existence, Yao's uniqueness, White's regularity sharpened by Guo, the Bernstein-Wang theory of the expander space. The reading's first correction was to the skim's shape: the load-bearing objects are not the two theorems but the three cited pillars under them, and the filing's grain followed the proof's dependencies, not the abstract's emphasis — existence, uniqueness and regularity stand as concepts in their own right because the argument spends each at several points. The second correction was a wording the paper leaves at war with itself: Theorem 1.1's statement says the link is diffeomorphic to a sphere, while its abstract and its own section 3 say a topological sphere. The filing states the theorem as its statement reads and opens the difference as Q137 rather than silently choosing — the discipline Paper 025's Q133 taught. And one fold was a judgment, recorded as one: the vanishing of the relative homotopy groups is Theorem 1.2's corollary, derived from its retraction and never used apart, so it files inside the retraction's concept; filed split, the Index would stand at 0.0340 instead of 0.0315, and the delta records the alternative so the choice can be re-priced.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster AJ is founded — the book's first differential geometry, its first extrinsic geometry of any kind: twelve concepts on ground where the model held not even the level-zero words (mean curvature had never occurred in the tracker; four foundations and the defined word 'link' had to be laid before the concepts could stand). The founding's bridges are three shared words — entropy, curvature, minimal — each naming a different quantity on the new ground than on the old, and each priced as shape at the founding, so that a later paper which makes one of them machinery will be visible as the event it is. The filing also crossed a line the arithmetic had set: 381 canonical concepts, over the 380 trigger, so Restructure Pass #19 ran inside the taking-in — all 381 reviewed, three merge candidates weighed and held apart, no endings, as the no-pruning rule requires. The registry stands re-proved in Lean at 1,035 entries; the phextura replay reconciles its transaction-log Index with the canonical 0.0315 to the last digit; and the Singular audit closes at zero. What the model can do now that it could not yesterday: receive a paper whose object is known only through a canonical resolution — and it holds a country's worth of ground on which the next such paper lands as an extension instead of a founding.
# Journal 21 — Quadratic Convexity for Closed and Free Boundary High Codimension Mean Curvature Flow — Huy The Nguyen, Artemis A. Vogiatzi

