Entry 22 — Paper 029: 'Data Just Wants to Be Format-Neutral' by Steven Pemberton (CWI, Amsterdam), Proc. XML Prague 2016, pp. 109-120. Written 2026-11-19, in the same pass as the filing. The twenty-second entry of the Thirst — and the first that stands outside the ratified protocol's twelve-paper sample altogether: no slot, no seal, no score.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. It was not chosen by the quest at all. Will posted the link bare in the MOAT chat at 13:56, no words around it, and the channel rule did the rest: a paper Will posts is a tracker ingestion. The protocol position had to be settled before the filing, and the preregistration settled it: the Will-posted stratum closed at four with Paper 027 and the cron stratum at five with Paper 028, so a fifth Will-posted paper has no stratum to enter. It files as a non-sample ingestion — its Novelty Index computed from the filed delta and entered in the ledger as every paper's is, its axis judgment written at filing under the charter's rule for Will-posted papers, and the sample left standing at nine of twelve with the far stratum still the scan's to fill.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The book is a mathematics book that had quietly become a book about whatever Will reads; this paper made that explicit. It is not mathematics proper — it is a formats paper, a position with worked constructions — and the filing neither apologised for that nor forced it into a mathematical mould: each claim priced by its own basis, construction where the paper builds, argument where it reasons from its witness, assertion where it only says. Its centre of gravity — grammars, parse trees, the neutrality of representations — was ground the tracker did not hold, and the charter's own tests said so: cluster AK founded, the format country, with the neighbours (G's translation, F's judgments, I and X's specifications, E's instrument-relative identity) priced as analogies of shape, not kin. The axis judgment followed Paper 027's form to the letter: the field-with-no-slot fact recorded plainly against the judgment, and the judgment still no — the paper's moves are of kinds the model holds, carried by objects it did not.

THE PASS INSIDE THE TAKING-IN. The filing's ten concepts carried the count to 404, over the 400 line, so Restructure Pass #20 fell due inside the taking-in and ran there: all 404 concepts reviewed, three merge candidates weighed and held apart with their grounds written down (a procedure against its product, an instrument against the property it serves, a corollary against its parent claim), the twin-check run against the held book, the areas audited. The pass also caught the house's own drift: the registry's claims for the narrative, the source index, the concept map and the researchers area had been left one paper stale by Paper 028's taking-in, and the area evidence chains carried no clause for it. The repair was made at this pass and dated to this pass — the chains now say the Paper 028 entries stand as added at their taking-in — rather than backdated into a history that did not happen.

THE MIRROR. The paper's claim is that a text and its parse tree are the same data in different clothing, and that conversion is parsing with one grammar and serialising with another. The house runs that claim as routine and did again within the hour: the canonical phext and its phextura shadow, converted, absorbed as a native transaction, compared scroll for scroll with no difference outside the two scrolls that are different by design. The filing's synthesis says the kinship plainly and labels it synthesis. What the process taught, again: a tracker that prices representations for a living should expect its papers to start pricing the tracker.
# Journal 23 — The Minimal Background Period for Infinitely Many Spectral Gaps in Anderson–Bernoulli Models — Cong Chen, Yong Li

Paper: The Minimal Background Period for Infinitely Many Spectral Gaps in Anderson–Bernoulli Models — Cong Chen, Yong Li
arXiv: arXiv:2611.22703 (math.SP)
Absorbed: 2026-11-20

Entry 23 — Paper 030: 'The Minimal Background Period for Infinitely Many Spectral Gaps in Anderson–Bernoulli Models' by Cong Chen, Yong Li (arXiv:2611.22703, math.SP). Written 2026-11-20, in the same pass as the filing. The twenty-third entry of the Thirst, the tenth slot of the ratified protocol's twelve-paper sample, and the first of the far stratum — the stratum the test was built to be able to lose on.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. Not from today's listing at all, and that is the protocol working as written. The cron and Will-posted strata closed last week (Papers 028 and 027); what remained was the far stratum, whose picks are chosen mechanically from the window's scan records: fewest held clusters touched, ties by scan order. Today's scan (674 entries; QA, RT, KT, OA, FA read in depth through the API) preserved its own far candidate — Xiao & Yu's proof of Casselman's comparison conjecture, zero touches, earliest of today's zero-touch papers — and the pool accounting then ran over every preserved candidate in order: the 14th, 16th and 17th scans bottomed at one touch; the 18th was the first scan with a zero-touch paper at its head, Chen & Li's Anderson–Bernoulli paper, declared that scan's mechanical far candidate three days before its seal existed. So the far stratum's first pick was made by the record, not by taste: a spectral-theory paper at maximal distance from every cluster the tracker holds. The preserved order for the remaining far slots now stands in today's scan record: the Airy_1 paper next, then Casselman.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the abstract alone: a minimality theorem (period three is the smallest background period that can tear an almost-sure spectrum into infinitely many gaps), an explicit period-three construction, robustness on an open parameter set, and a named proof route — heteroclinic connection, invariant multicones, periodic realizations, a strict geometric window, exact rational verification. The seal priced eight concepts, NI 0.0194, no revisions, axis at 0.15 (a founding expected; the precedents say founding is not an axis), ten internal links against one cross, three questions. It also named its own risk in advance: on far ground, with no held cluster to price the spread from, the grain rule would surface load-bearing objects the abstract does not enumerate.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract's five named instruments each arrived with a second object standing behind it. The connection came with its transversality — a Wronskian identity, δ′(E*) equal to the squared ℓ² norm of the joined eigenfunction, load-bearing at three separate points of the argument. The multicones came with a finite boundary-transition graph and a decreasing integer rank, which is what turns weak invariance into strict invariance after exactly m+n+1 words. The periodic realizations came with two distinct theorems where the skim had priced one: a common-spectrum result (every finite noise word occurs, phase-aligned, arbitrarily far out, so one spectrum serves every Bernoulli law) standing apart from the exclusion result that keeps the constructed energies out of it. And the exact rational verification turned out to be a complete appendix of interval arithmetic — matrix entries enclosed by endpoint evaluation, fixed directions located by sign changes of two quadratics, the connection's sign change certified through an auxiliary polynomial, and the scale window reduced, at the last step, to two integer comparisons a child could check: 57·4993162 − 10·28394391 = 666324 > 0, and a companion seventeen times larger. The house checked: both comparisons recompute exactly, and four sampled fixed-direction evaluations match the paper's fractions to the digit. That verification is filed as a verified leg — the far paper, improbably, exercised the True gate harder than most near papers do.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster AL founded — spectral theory of random operators, ground the book had never held: fourteen new concepts (1.2.405–1.2.418), four foundations and a defined word ('gap'), four stated state machines (159 in all), questions to Q152, narrative at v43. The filing's shape is the lesson: at the protocol's grain, the paper's own results enumerate nine objects and its proof machinery contributes five more, because the instrument rule and the split rule keep firing — the Wronskian stands apart from the connection it makes transverse, the common spectrum stands apart from the exclusion it serves. No held claim moved; no cross-cluster link was honestly groundable and none was filed — the dynamics and cone kinships to held ground were priced as shape, as the last two foundings priced them.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED AND WHY. On the headline grain the skim undercounted by six (8 foretold, 14 filed) and the Index missed its interval on the high side (0.0335 against [0.012, 0.029]) — the worst count miss of the sample, on the stratum where the retrospective record said accuracy would collapse. The cause is named and singular: not aim, but enumeration depth. The abstract of a far paper lists its results honestly and its machinery partially; five of the six missed concepts are machinery the abstract compresses into a single clause ('invariant multicones' carries the boundary graph and its rank inside it, unpriced). At the coarser sensitivity grain the seal foresaw the filing almost exactly — 12 filed against 8 foretold is still a miss, but the Index lands inside its interval there (0.0288), which says the skim read the paper's size rightly and the protocol's grain priced the rest, the Paper 028 pattern at twice the distance. The nulls tell the same story from the other side: on counts the expanding-mean null (error 1.9) beat the skim outright, while on the Index the skim beat the flat and global-mean nulls and lost only to that same expanding mean. The axis call held (no axis; Brier 0.0225 — worse than the flat null's 0.0025, the price of honestly pricing a far founding at 0.15 instead of parroting the base rate). Links ran dense again inside the new cluster (16 against 10 foretold) and the one priced cross link never landed. Rolling skim error rose, 0.0123 to 0.0137: the far stratum's first datum, entered without flinching, exactly where the test said it would hurt.
