Entry 29 — Paper 036 — arXiv:2611.25367, Xiao & Yu, A proof of Casselman's comparison conjecture; far pick 3 of 3, sample slot 12 of 12 — the paper that closes the pre-registered sample. Choosing: again no choice was exercised — the mechanical far rule named this paper in the scan of 2026-11-20 and the preserved pool order carried it through two further scans untouched; the 2026-11-24 scan (1,396 listings) was run in full because the scan record is the far stratum's evidence. Skim: from the abstract the skim foresaw a founding at the low end of the founding range (12 concepts), zero revisions with two watch items named, zero cross-cluster links, an axis at 0.15 against, and the ledger holding at the corrected ~75% — and the filing landed inside both sealed intervals, with questions exact at three and revisions exact at zero. Reading changed the count, not the shape: the Casselman-Jacquet functor, named only in the abstract's keywords, turned out to be the proof's central instrument and earned its own scroll under the instrument rule, and the Bruhat/normal filtration machine of Sections 3-4, invisible in the abstract, filed as the orbit comparison — 14 concepts against 12 foretold, the standing founding pattern in its mildest form yet (+2, where the earlier far foundings ran +4 and worse). Integration: cluster AR founded (representation theory of real reductive groups), no restructure pass fired, registry to 1,299 with the re-proof launched on the hub, the phextura shadow validated green, and the Singular audit brought to residual 0 — including a 57-word stale backlog the audit had been carrying since Paper 035, cleared into the word lists at this pass rather than left for the next. Where the prediction failed: links, for once, in the other direction (+2 over — the seal priced in the founding under-read and overshot), and the axis Brier paid its standing tax (0.0225 against the flat nulls' 0.0025/0.0014) on a sample that never produced an axis-positive paper. And the day's real result is the sample's verdict, computed by script against the criteria fixed on 2026-11-14 before any of these papers were read: NI false (ratio 1.04 against the best null), counts false (MAE ratio 1.48, signed bias +4.56 — every scored paper filed over its sealed count), intervals false (4 of 9 scored), axis untested (zero axis-positive papers arrived), overall FALSE. The skim was not lying; it was, measurably, a little worse than predictors that read nothing on the pooled Index race, and systematically optimistic on counts in one direction. That is what a pre-registered test is for: the claim can lose, and it did, in writing, with the numbers kept.
