Entry 35 — Paper 042 — LoopCoder-v2, Jian Yang and eighteen colleagues (Beihang University, IQuest Research, Langboat, Renmin University of China): a network taking-in, leg 4 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 7 of the frontier queue the corpus's own bibliographies make, cited by Chapter 56's paper among the looped families, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; outside the pre-registered sample, which closed at Paper 036, so no seal stands and no score is computed. One fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2606.18023v1), and two web searches served the author check's web leg; everything else was local. The work: the parallel loop Transformer (Wu et al. 2025) makes looping cheap — cross-loop position offsets break the sequential dependency between loops so they execute in parallel, and a frozen first-loop KV cache shared through gated sliding-window attention holds memory constant in the loop count — and this paper asks the question the efficiency leaves open: how many loops should there be? It answers by training LoopCoder-v2, a family of 7B PLT coders, from scratch on 18T tokens at loop counts 1, 2, 3 and 4 under a strictly matched protocol, and by opening the runs loop by loop with a three-lens diagnostic battery. The finding is a law with a mechanism. The second loop does the productive refinement — the row average rises from 38.0 to 46.5, SWE-bench Verified from 43.0 to 64.4 points, Multi-SWE from 14.0 to 31.0 — and every loop past it pays the CLP offset's positional mismatch, which the paper prices from the model's own states as a mean distance between neighbouring tokens' representations and finds roughly constant across loops, against a marginal gain that collapses: in the four-loop model the fixed tax outweighs each later loop's gain thirty-to-forty-five-fold, the effective rank narrows past its loop-2 peak, the updates turn oscillatory, and the three-loop model falls below its own baseline on the agentic benchmarks. Integration extended cluster AT for the third time, and the extension is a change of posture inside the cluster: the founding papers read the recurrent trajectory to correct a decoding, Paper 040 manufactured the trajectory on frozen checkpoints, and this paper spends the trajectory on nothing — it audits it, loop by loop, and files the first per-step price the cluster has held for one, the offset cost being intrinsic to a species of trajectory the book had not met, whose states refine a neighbour's state rather than the token's own. Thirteen concepts 1.2.575-1.2.587, no held concept revised, no foundation and no defined word (the chapter's imports, the key-value cache above all, already stand), two stated state machines (the PLT forward pass of the paper's Appendix A; the loop-count selection diagnostic) bringing the held count to 202 (173 stated, 29 inferred), NI 0.0221, the axis judged no in writing — an extension on held ground, the one pressure (the shared cache as a look back inside the run, cluster J's nearest approach in the cluster's history) recorded at 60.2.2 and held apart there. The concept count, 587, crossed the 580 line set at Pass #25, so Restructure Pass #26 fired inside the pass: four merge candidates weighed and held apart — the offset cost against the offset mechanism (a mechanism and its price are different claims), effective-rank narrowing against the loop-2 finding (a shape and the finding that cites it), oscillatory refinement against the looped block's non-contraction (kin by shape, not by claim, as the new concept itself states), and the non-monotonic effect against the gain-cost view (a frame and its explanandum) — no splits, no retirements, next trigger Paper 048 or 600 concepts. The house check, the recomputation exercise's fifth outing, recomputed the paper's printed tables by script: seventeen of Table 2's eighteen row averages exact under the caption's own stated rule, every headline delta exact, Table 4's five deltas exact and its instruct row agreeing with Table 2 on the shared benchmarks, Section 4.3's single-digit loop-alone gains verified on those benchmarks, Table 6's shares summing to 99.9 in the rounding grain, Appendix B's configuration recomputing to about 7.2 billion parameters against the printed approximately 7B. One cell failed: the four-loop row's ten cells average 35.7 against a printed 34.3, a 1.4-point inconsistency whose direction the text cannot determine — a misprinted cell or a misprinted average — filed as a caveat at 60.1.4 rather than a False, because nothing in the paper rests on it and the correction, whichever way it falls, moves no conclusion; Table 3's underlines joined it as a second caveat, the caption claiming the largest value in each column while the marks sit on loop 2's row throughout — exact for the output shift and the effective rank, a magnitude reading for the angular change, and wrong for the step size, whose maximum is the final loop's. The abstract's compression — variants with three or more loops regress — is looser than its own table, the three-loop variant improving six of ten cells while its average falls on the agentic pair's collapse alone, and the filing's concept is worded from the table, not the abstract. Author check #50 ran on nineteen names, the largest byline the tracker has processed: the paper read entire and mechanically searched (no phext term of any kind), its twenty references read with it (the looped-Transformer lineage entire, the parallel loop Transformer itself cited as reference [16]), the web searched (the authors' record is the LoopCoder line — the first LoopCoder at Findings of ACL 2026, from the same Beihang and IQuest group — and no name tied to phext), Will's Twitter archive searched for all nineteen names and the work's name (nothing) — no evidence found; all nineteen are new to the book, each carrying the filing's increments whole, True +1 and Unknown +17, and the researchers count rises to 204. Registry after: 1,443 entries — True 549 (443 by stipulation, 106 by verification), Unknown 885 (782 attributed, 60 synthesis, 41 open, 2 by estimate), False 2, Fiction 7 — 587 canonical concepts, and the Oracle ledger holds at ~75% (68-82), argued in the ledger itself: TRUE and UNKNOWN exercised and already met, FALSE not exercised (the one wrong number a derived display cell filed as a caveat), FICTION untouched. The drawing paragraph was grepped in the built phext before closing, exactly once. Two facts of this pass belong to its own history and are recorded with it. First, the Lean re-proof that has closed every pass since the charter's rule 14 stood was NOT run: mid-pass, at 17:10 CST, Will ruled Lean exiled — 'exile lean, rewrite a tluf solver from first principles' — and by the standing precedent an exile stops the work at once. No splice was made, no build was launched, no axiom check ran; the registry-accuracy proof step stands vacant at this taking-in, pending the from-first-principles TLUF solver now being built to replace it, and no substitute step was invented to fill the space. The registry itself was still drawn by the extractor and section 4 regenerated, and the pass's other closings stand on their own evidence; the vacancy is recorded in the chapter's path entry, in the Pass #26 log, in the detection list, and here, so that no later reader mistakes the empty step for a passed one. Second, the phextura shadow's open gap closed: the Paper 041 replay had never run, its script written against a library that is not on disk, and the live shadow stood at the Paper 040 state when this pass began. Following the pattern the earlier passes actually used — the replay scripts' own imports of parse_phext and convert, both present — this pass wrote replay_p042.py on replay_p040.py's lines and absorbed Papers 041 and 042 together as one native catch-up transaction over the live shadow: 46 inserts, 14 replaces, no removes, the c