Entry 36 — Paper 043 — LOTUS: Bridging the Gap Between Latent and Explicit Reasoning with Looped Transformers, Ying Fan (University of Wisconsin-Madison; Microsoft Research), Anej Svete (ETH Zürich) and Kangwook Lee (University of Wisconsin-Madison; KRAFTON; Ludo Robotics): a network taking-in, leg 5 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 8 of the frontier queue the corpus's own bibliographies make, cited by Chapter 56's paper among the latent-reasoning methods, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; outside the pre-registered sample, which closed at Paper 036, so no seal stands and no score is computed. One fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2606.31779v1, appendices included), and two web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local. A v2 stands on arXiv (13 July 2026); the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do, and the caveat at 61.1.4 owns the choice. The work names its phenomenon before its method: latent chain-of-thought matches explicit CoT on small backbones and falls behind beyond a billion parameters, the shortfall widening as the backbone grows — CODI + SIM-CoT level at GPT-2 and 9.2 points behind at 3B — and LOTUS closes that gap by grounding the latents directly. A looped padded Transformer holds K blocks of c latent tokens between question and answer, block i pinned by position to gold CoT step i, refined for R iterations under an input-injected recurrence that re-adds the latent embeddings at every pass and attends throughout to the question's prefix cache, computed once. The grounding is a step loss: every post-loop latent position scored by cross-entropy, through the base model's own LM head, against its step's gold token — direct, parallel, post-loop — beside the answer loss, the two dividing as coverage and selection in the paper's own analysis: the step loss optimizes a parallel chain likelihood whose dropped autoregres