Entry 38 — Paper 045 — LaDiR: Kang, Zhang, Kuang, Majamaki, Jaitly, Ma & Qin (University of California, San Diego, and Apple), 'LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning': a network taking-in, leg 7 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 10 of the frontier queue the corpus's own bibliographies make, cited in Chapter 56's bibliography, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; the record carried no identifier, so the paper was identified by title and authors in one web search, and one fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2510.04573v1, appendices A-G included); two further web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local. A version 6 stands (23 April 2026) and the work appears at ICLR 2026; the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do, and the caveat at 63.1.4 owns the choice. The work asks whether a reasoning step can be formed rather than emitted. First the space: a beta-VAE — encoder the backbone LLM fine-tuned, decoder the same model frozen — encodes each sentence of a text chain-of-thought as one block of latent thought tokens, robust to the imperfect latents a generator produces. Then the generation: the reasoning model produces the blocks in sequence, causal across blocks and bidirectional within, each block denoised from Gaussian noise along a straight-line path under a velocity field trained by flow matching, until a classification head at each block's end predicts the stop and the answer is generated conditioned on the blocks. Then the evidence: on seven math benchmarks the best average pass@1 of the ten methods compared; on Countdown — the whole reasoning compressed into a single four-token block — the best Pass@100 and diversity at both sizes; and, printed from decodes of the forming blocks themselves, a thought visibly correcting its own arithmetic as the denoising proceeds, stable and correct from t = 0.25 with no check