Entry 40 — Paper 047 — RLTT: Williams & Tureci (Princeton University), 'Prioritize the Process, Not Just the Outcome: Rewarding Latent Thought Trajectories Improves Reasoning in Looped Language Models': a network taking-in, leg 9 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 12 of the frontier queue, cited in Chapter 56's bibliography, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; the record carried no identifier, so the paper was identified by title and authors in one web search, and one fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2602.10520v1, appendices A.1-A.9 included); two further web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local — four network operations in all. A version 2 stands (12 February 2026), a version 3 (28 May 2026, its experiments revised — a second scale and different headline gains) and the ICML 2026 proceedings text (PMLR 306); the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do, and the caveat at 65.1.4 owns the choice. One correction to the frontier's own record was filed with the paper: the queue named three authors, but the byline of the version held carries two — Jonathan Williams and Esin Tureci — with Olga Russakovsky thanked in v1's acknowledgments and standing as third author of the proceedings version; the filing follows the held text, credits novelty to the v1 byline, and records her position precisely at 65.1.4 and 65.1.6, exposure to the record and authorship of the version being different findings. The work asks why outcome-reward reinforcement learning, which works on ordinary language models, had failed on looped ones — the Ouro authors themselves report RL bringing no gains over SFT — and answers with a credit-assignment diagnosis: the standard objective rewards the terminal loop only, while the architecture computes a whole latent trajectory per token, so the learning signal treats a multi-step computation as a single decision. RLTT is the repair built from the diagnosis: keep the one binary outcome reward a rollout earns, and distribute it across every loop of the latent thought distribution trajectory by a loop-weighted policy gradient — uniform, progressive, or exit-probability weighting, the last crediting each loop the probability of the model's own learned exit head — the group-normalized advantage unchanged, a KL term to the frozen reference policy added. The evidence: trained on MATH alone on Ouro-2.6B-Thinking, RLTT beats GRPO on all eight benchmarks, the mathematical average up 18.8 points and GPQA nearly doubling on training that never saw a non-math problem; the responses shortening as they improve with no brevity term anywhere in the reward; entropy falling in tandem and acquitted of collapse by the Pass@k analysis; the advantage persisting across decode budgets and at every loop count, already large at one and two loops; the gradient signal measurably richer exactly on the hardest benchmarks; and a theory appendix that prices the shortening — trajectory credit pays a strictly larger per-token uncertainty cost than terminal credit, and a larger cost selects a weakly shorter optimal length. Integration extended cluster AT — the trajectory as its own reference — an eighth paper, its second training-time member after Paper 043 and its first reinforcement-learning one, and the extension was argued against the neighbours the paper actually touches: AV held apart, because nothing denoises and no thought space is learned — the backbone is a looped transformer and the trajectory is the recurrence itself, walked at training time; AU, AP and AC held apart in their turns; and the J crossing condition stated at 62.2.2, weighed at 63.2.2 and again at 64.2.2, was weighed a fourth time and not crossed — the outcome reward is read off the emitted answer against the answer key, it inspects the output text and never a latent state, and it returns one bit for the whole rollout rather than a verdict on any loop, while the trajectory-level machinery consults nothing beyond the trajectory at all; the paper's own contrast is with LSRL, which decodes intermediate states and has an external model grade them — that is a consulted reference, and it is the thing RLTT is built to not need — and the holding is written falsifiably, in the condition's own terms: a member whose per-loop credit were set by a scorer reading the loops crosses, and Q222 keeps the seam open. Twelve concepts 1.2.635-1.2.646, no held concept revised, no foundation laid, no word coined, two stated state machines (the RLTT training machine; the LoopLM generation machine) bringing the held count to 212 (183 stated, 29 inferred), NI 0.0186, the axis judged no in writing: an eighth paper inside the founding cluster, its act new to the cluster but its ground surveyed, is distance covered in known ground. Restructuring Pass #29 fell due at this filing — the concept count reaching 646 crosses the 640-concept line set at Pass #28, the paper trigger (Paper 054) not reached — and is logged at 1.1.5: five merge candidates weighed and all held apart, the mechanism from its framework, the scheme from the instance that reads the model's own halting signal, the token axis from the loop axis, the entropy finding from the length finding, and the disease from the repair; the next pass falls due at Paper 057 or 660 canonical concepts. The house check recomputed the printed arithmetic by script — eighty checks, seventy-six exact: all sixteen of Table 2's row averages and every printed gain, the abstract's three among them; the cross-table identities whole — Table 3's 2048 column cell-for-cell Table 2's MATH-500 column, Table 5's exit-probability row Table 2's RLTT row entire, Table 9's four-loop column Table 2 again with twelve of its thirteen stated deltas exact; Table 10's GSNR directions as the prose reads them; Table 1's GRPO row reconciling and its relative cost exact; and Appendix A.9's exhibited computations exact, the octagon's 6564 among them. Four checks failed, all carried as caveats at 65.1.4 with no False: Appendix A.6's prose pricing MATH-500's two-loop improvement at +14.4 where its own table gives 15.0, the paragraph's other two deltas exact, so the middle number stands against its table by 0.6 and the filing does not choose; Table 1's RLTT row not reconciling at its printed grain, 21.1 minutes over the stated 140 steps making 49.23 hours against the printed 49.05, the row's conclusion holding under both readings; the same table's '10.0% reduction' headline matching neither its totals' 9.87% nor its means' 9.44%; and Appendix A.3's summary — that weighting variation is small relative to the RLTT-GRPO gap across all benchmarks — failing on MBPP, where the three weightings span 5.5 points against a margin of 3.3, the exception recorded rather than the False because the claim is a summary of a table the paper prints in full and the table is exact. Author check #55 found no evidence: the paper read entire and scanned — no phext term of any kind — its references read with it, the built tracker scanned for all three names the queue record carries, the archive searched entire, the web searched twice, no tie anywhere; Williams and Tureci are new to the book, each carrying True +1 and Unknown +16 from this filing, and the researchers count rises to 221. Two precisions the check exists to keep: the book's Williamses are Harold Williams and Lauren Williams, other people, recorded apart at 65.1.6; and the third name's position — thanked in the version filed, an author of the proceedings version — is stated exactly, so the frontier's record and the paper's are both on the page. Registry after: 1,549 entries — True 561 (445 by stipulation, 116 by verification), Unknown 979 (876 attributed, 60 synthesis, 41 open, 2 by estimate), False 2, Fiction 7 — 646 canonical concepts, and the Oracle ledger holds at ~75% (68-82): TRUE exercised and met, UNKNOWN exercised and met, FALSE not exercised, FICTION untouched. The registry-proof step closed clean: the TLUF solver printing ACCURATE on check and ACCURATE on preserve, 21 additions, no transitions, 1,528 unchanged, assumptions: none. The phextura shadow absorbed the filing natively (base sync clean; 21 inserts, 14 replaces, no removes; every comparable scroll identical to the direct conversion; validate.py GREEN entire, run against this pass's own conversion report) and stands advanced. The drawing paragraph, the chapter heading and the Pass #29 log entry were each grepped in the built phext, exactly once each. Two facts of this pass belong to its own history. First, the walk has now weighed the J condition four times without a crossing, and the seam it guards has sharpened each time: Chapter 62's probe read a verdict off the trajectory, Chapter 64's fusion spent the trajectory's signal as material, Chapter 63's gold chain supervised it from outside, and this chapter's reward lands on the trajectory whole without inspecting it — the map now holds every relation a signal can bear to the trajectory it shapes, and Q222 asks the question the four holdings jointly pose: whether sharpness can be borrowed across the seam without changing a signal's kind. Second, a defect of the pass itself, owned: the first integration script was written from the checkpoint summary's shape of the builder rather than from the previous pass's script, and its concept-insert marker matched nothing; it failed on its own assertion before writing anything, the builder never carried a partial state, and the script was rewritten from integrate_p046.py line for line — the P044 lesson in a second form, that a pass's texts take the house's shape from the house's artifacts, not from a summary of them. Q222-Q225 open: whether whole-trajectory credit systematically reinforces degenerate paths, and whether a per-loop signal could sharpen it without becoming a consulted reference; whether the weighting insensitivity is a property of the 