Entry 50 — Paper 057 — OlympiadBench: A Challenging Benchmark for Promoting AGI with Olympiad-Level Bilingual Multimodal Scientific Problems: He and thirteen co-authors — leg eighteen of the network walk, queue rank 21 of the reference frontier, cited by Chapter 56 (LoopCD) in its evaluation tables. The queue record carried the title with 'Science Problems'; identified by title in one search to arXiv:2402.14008 — the filing reads the second version entire (2024-06-06, the ACL 2024 update, proceedings pages 3828-3850) under the held text's own title, which reads 'Scientific Problems'; the first version stands unfiled. Network operations were few and at human pace under the netizen rule: the paper fetched once in its arXiv HTML form (230,781 bytes held, sha256 272aac77…), extracted to text (70,096 characters) and read whole, appendices included; the dataset held entire — the benchmark's Hugging Face home (Hothan/OlympiadBench) yielding the card and eighteen parquet configurations, some ninety-nine megabytes with the images embedded, downloaded one file at a time with pauses, after the GitHub repository proved to hold code only and no data; one arXiv API call resolved three untitled queue records for the frontier's hygiene; two web searches served the author check. The work: OlympiadBench, from Tsinghua, Beihang and the Wisdom Way AI Lab — a benchmark artifact of 8,476 olympiad-level problems, mathematics 6,142 and physics 2,334, English 2,125 and Chinese 6,351, 4,869 with images, drawn from the international and Chinese olympiads and from Gaokao mock examinations, arranged as a designed difficulty gradient. Where Chapter 70's answer key was thirty problems and thirty integers with the solutions withheld, this table carries its apparatus: an expert step-by-step solution for every problem, a five-type answer contract (numerical, expression, equation, tuple, interval — with compound types on multi-answer rows), hand annotations of subfield and answer type across twenty-two subfields, chained progressive problems for physics whose shared scenario rides in a context field, and a shipped automatic scoring pipeline that preprocesses, matches exactly, compares intervals and tuples elementwise, compares numbers within a per-problem tolerance, normalizes equations, and tests expressions by SymPy simplification. Its headline measurement: GPT-4V averages 17.97% on the open-ended problems — 21.70 in mathematics, 10.74 in physics. Integration extended cluster AY — THE ANSWER KEY: THE MEASURE AS A FIXED TABLE — its second paper: the founding at Chapter 70 was made at the level of the artifact class, and this is one, a fixed table of problems and verdicts published as data; the apparatus that distinguishes it from the bare key is furniture, filed as concepts, and a cluster founded on the furniture would split the country by degree rather than kind. Cluster J was weighed — the walk's fourth weighing since the crossing, and the closest since Chapter 71 — and NOT joined: the scoring pipeline is a mechanism and it consults a reference distinct from the judged output, the stored final answers; but the crossing condition turns on where the verdict is spent, and Chapter 71 crossed because the verdict was consumed inside the training loop as reward while the judged computation was still running, whereas this pipeline spends its verdicts at grading, after the judged computation has ended — the AY pattern itself. Stated falsifiably in the chapter: a benchmark whose grader fed verdicts back into the judged run would cross; this one's does not. 1.2.716 remains J's only resident mechanism. Twelve concepts 1.2.757-1.2.768 file the work at the instrument's grain — OlympiadBench itself, the progressive problem, the five-type answer contract, the automatic scoring pipeline, the expert solution annotation, the bilingual split, the difficulty gradient, leakage minimization by transcription, embedding deduplication, the subfield annotation, the unavailable-response removal, and incorrect judging — no held concept revised and none merged. Restructuring Pass #35 fell due in this taking-in, the count crossing the 760 line set at Pass #34: five merges weighed and held apart, each on its record (the scoring pipeline into the exact-match verdict it generalizes; the solution annotation into the answer key that withholds solutions; the five-type contract into the integer contract whose single-type discipline it relaxes; embedding deduplication into transcription, the curation machine's opposite act; incorrect judging into the unavailable-response removal, the numerator's corruption against the denominator's shrinkage), no splits, none retired, the next pass falling due at Paper 060 or 780 canonical concepts. No foundation was laid and no word was coined: the paper's own names (progressive problem, answer type, incorrect judging) carry its devices. Three state machines, all stated in the text — the curation machine, whose named gaps are the unstated deduplication threshold and the unmeasured manual-verification error rate; the automatic scoring machine, whose gaps are the unsized incorrect-judging class and the theorem-proving rows that never enter it; and the evaluation machine, whose gap is the silent denominator, removal counts printed nowhere — bringing the held count to 234 (199 stated, 35 inferred), NI 0.0156. The house check ran by declaration in the pre-processed shape: a checkfile of one hundred thirty-seven declarations through the pre-processor's harness — one hundred thirty-seven pass, none at tension, none failing — against two sources, the held text and the held artifact itself, the dataset counted row by row by a kept script. The census confirms the paper's account of its artifact exactly: 8,476 rows with every Table 2 split (subjects, languages, question types, modality, difficulty) and all twenty-two of Table 9's subfield counts matching one for one; every row carrying a solution; the answer-type census closing on the 6,728 open-ended rows; and Table 4's headline averages recomputing to the printed hundredth as micro-averages over the artifact's own subset sizes — GPT-4V's 21.70, 10.74 and 17.97, the text-only block's 31.01, 32.00, 16.24 and 16.24, DeepSeekMath's 18.09. Five caveats stand at 75.1.4, each verified in exact shape: Table 1 lists the benchmark's question type as open-ended only, omitting the 1,748 theorem-proving problems its own Table 2 counts; Table 5's competition counts sum to 2,403, matching neither the released English competition rows (2,125) nor all competition rows (2,869), its population nowhere stated — the artifact's ids spanning 0 to 8,976 for 8,476 rows, five hundred numbers spent on rows the release does not contain; Table 9's Gaokao and competition group sums for mathematics (4,457 and 1,685) do not equal the artifact's difficulty-field split (4,009 and 2,133), though every subfield count and both totals are exact — the headings group thematically and the rows cross them; Table 8's long/normal split sums to 231, matching no partition of the released Physics-En_COMP subsets, and its rows micro-average to 12.99 and 11.44 rather than Table 4's 12.71 and 12.29; and the text's 'more than 5 times larger than the best-performing open-source model (Yi-VL-34B)' divides as 5.25 against the named model but 4.92 against LLaVA-NeXT-34B's 3.65, the table's own best open-source average — the sentence survives only by naming the second-best as best. They are filed as caveats, not False filings, on the standing distinction: the registry's two False filings are load-bearing claims of a proof, refuted at the claim's own level; these are summaries and groupings against an artifact that prints the true values beside them. What stands attributed: every model's accuracy as a measurement, the manual theorem-proving sample of section 5.1 (6 of 81), and Table 7's borrowed MATH and MathVista numbers; the house recomputed the arithmetic among printed numbers and the artifact's