Entry 53 — Paper 060 — Looped Transformers are Better at Learning Learning Algorithms: Yang, Lee, Nowak & Papailiopoulos — leg twenty-one of the network walk, queue rank 24 of the reference frontier, cited by Chapter 56, whose own concept (1.2.680, input injection) this paper authored. The queue record carried the title and a surname but no identifier and no authors; identified by title in one search — 'Looped Transformers are Better at Learning Learning Algorithms', arXiv:2311.12424 — and held entire in its v3 form (16 Mar 2024; v1 of 21 Nov 2023; ICLR 2024): the arXiv HTML (348,744 bytes) and its text layer (81,456 characters) held beside it, one fetch at human pace under the netizen rule. The work: the University of Wisconsin–Madison group's looped transformer as an in-context solver — a single GPT-2 block (D 256, 8 heads; 0.79M parameters against the 9.48M of the 12-layer standard transformer it is compared to) applied again and again to its own output, the embedded prompt re-injected at every step, and trained not on its final iterate but on a truncated window of its late iterates, until the loop is an iterative algorithm and its fixed point is the prompt's fit: gradient descent for linear regression, a Lasso-like iteration for the sparse problem, and learned solvers for a depth-4 decision tree and a two-layer ReLU network, each reached by the same block with no task-specific part. The skim foresaw the home before the full reading: cluster AT, the looped country, was the presumption of the brief itself, and the reading confirmed it and gave it its weight — this is the country's ancestor, the 2023 construction from which the modern looped line descends, filed as its fourteenth paper after its descendants, carrying the device (input injection) that Paper 050 imported from it by name and the exit-trained iteration that speaks to the depth-axis question, Q252, left open at Chapter 72. Integration extended cluster AT accordingly, and the extension adds the country's