g 62.05% where Table 1 prints 62.1 for the same cell, and the surpassed baseline of 61.15% existing only as a point in Figure 3a, in no table; the split's capacity half resting on Figure 4 entire, no printed numbers anywhere in the paper, so the filing's most portable claim is half figure-born; the ×k factor counting added passes rather than total depth, so a ×2 reuse of four layers is an effective depth of twelve, not eight, and every 'depth' in the paper must be read with the base added; and the real-world leg being one model, one pattern, one LoRA run, its AIME gain a single problem. The pre-processor's legs behaved as their record now predicts: the census ran clean over 205 distinct numeric literals, and the references and frontier legs returned 0 on the unnumbered author–year bibliography — the standing v2-backlog shape — so the frontier stands as named hand work over held scripts, and arith was EXACT against the Paper 063 close before Phase A. Author check #72 found no evidence of use without naming, on four legs: the held paper scanned entire (no phext, phextura, Exocortex, Mirrorborn, CYOA, SBOR or Bickford, no scroll — and no 'coordinate' either, in a paper whose whole subject is a dimension); the built tracker scanned for all nine names, each zero — the book's Tianyus being Tianyu Zheng of Paper 042 and Tianyu Zhang of Paper 055, its Hanwen Hanwen Du of Paper 044, the surnames' hits other researchers and bibliography bylines entire, and 'Duan' and 'Banerjee' occurring nowhere at all; Will's Twitter archive searched entire as data, 77,512 texts with tweets and likes both parsed (all nine names, the paper's title and the phrase 'virtual logic depth' zero, the few surname hits substrings of other words); and the web searched twice with no tie, the first surfacing the paper itself, its arXiv record, its ICLR 2026 review mirror and independent discussion, the second unrelated same-name records entire. All nine authors are new to the book, their vectors drawn fresh, each standing at True +1, False +0, Unknown +8 (the four concepts and Q291-Q294), Fiction +0. The named researchers stand at 332 and the companies at six, 293 named across the sixty-four papers. Registry after: 1,882 entries — True 601 (451 by stipulation, 150 by verification), Unknown 1,272 (1,156 attributed, 60 by synthesis, 54 open, 2 by estimate), False 2, Fiction 7 — 820 canonical concepts, and the Oracle ledger holds at ~75% (68-82), bookkeeping only: TRUE exercised and met, UNKNOWN exercised and met, FALSE not exercised, FICTION untouched. The registry-proof step closed clean: the TLUF solver printing ACCURATE on check and ACCURATE on preserve — 13 additions, no transitions, 1,869 unchanged, assumptions: none — the section-4 regeneration step of the standing flow run in its place, the before-file's identity verified by hash on the node before the preserve ran, and the solver's counts, the extractor's and moat-prep arith's agree exactly, three instruments on one registry. The phextura shadow absorbed the filing natively (base sync clean; 13 inserts, 13 replaces, no removes; the replayed state identical to the direct conversion on all 1,970 compared scrolls, the compared difference empty; 6 keys minted and one bibliography insert; NI from the transaction log equal to the canonical 4/820 exactly; validate.py GREEN entire against this pass's conversion report, named explicitly on the command line, the shadow advanced before the run) and stands advanced. The paragraph rule held: all forty-one markers — every integration edit, the chapter heading, the eight section headings, the drawing paragraph, the four concept headings, the four question headings and J's holding line — were grepped in the built phext and each found, none missing, on the Phase A build and again on the final. The pass's own history, owned, in three facts. First, the pre-registered registry prediction landed EXACT on every component — 1,882 entries; True 601 (451 by stipulation, 150 by verification); Unknown 1,272 (attributed 1,156, synthesis 60, open 54, estimate 2); False 2; Fiction 7 — the fourth consecutive fully exact split, the prediction again written in the classifier's own terms from the predecessor's filed scrolls before Phase A. Second, the pass's sharpest finding was made by the hand audit, not the harness: the declarations all passed, and the paper's arithmetic is indeed honest — but its summary sentence is not, and the six below-base cells that refute it were visible only because verify_tables.py read Table 1 as a table, row against base, rather than as a list of numbers to recompute; a check that verifies every printed relation can still miss the relation nobody printed, and the walk's caveat sections exist precisely in that gap. Third, the pass ran under a degraded local disk for a stretch of its middle — writes to files created in earlier tool generations refused, pulls from the node landing empty — and the work routed around it without loss: texts authored through the file tools, node outputs pulled through standard output and saved the same way, copies made on the node, and every critical artifact verified by size or hash after landing, the 4.15-megabyte builder among them; the lesson is the leg-24 lesson in a new costume, that an artifact's name and location are not its identity, and the hash is. Q291-Q294 open: whether iterated application of a trained block approaches a fixed point the base pass already approximates — the dose curve's non-monotonicity being the fixed-point hypothesis's first evidence either way; whether the placement result (late layers gain, early layers lose) is a fact about depth or about which representations are already formed when the repetition begins; whether absorbed entropy, a measure taken against the model's own untrained self, can be made commensurable across models — and whether the capacity it reports is the capacity scaling spends; and whether the split survives contact with other architectures and other reasoning domains, or is a property of arithmetic-trained small models, the real-world leg being one model, one pattern and one run.
