What the filing did to the book is to give cluster AR its eighth paper, and with it the theory of completions the cluster's seven earlier papers presuppose: 'globalization' entered the tracker as Paper 036's machinery — a smooth completion here, a Casselman-Wallach representation there — and this filing holds the theory whole, at the source the founding chapter itself cites for its broadest form. Twelve concepts were filed: the four canonical globalizations, the nested completions analytic, smooth, Hilbert, distribution, hyperfunction, each depending only on the infinitesimal equivalence class, with the theorem's engine filed inside the concept — a form extends to the small end of the chain and provably not to the large end unless the module is finite-dimensional; the unitarity problem, split at the outset into existence of an invariant Hermitian form and its positive-definiteness, posed as the gap between the admissible dual and the unitary dual; Casselman-Wallach norm canonicity, the technical heart, the measuring norms of a Hilbert globalization agreeing up to polynomial factors in the size of the K-type, without which 'canonical' would be a courtesy word; the sequence-space globalizations, the four completions read off a K-type expansion as exponentially decreasing, rapidly decreasing, slowly increasing and subexponential sequences, with SL(2,R) on the disc as the worked example where Taylor coefficients carry the whole chain; real parabolic induction in the compact picture, the Levi chosen by the Cartan involution, induction realized on functions on K so that globalization becomes regularity; the measurable complex partial flag variety, Wolf's finite orbits, the nice and very nice subalgebras, and measurability shown to be a real condition by the Grassmannian computation that finds an open orbit whose isotropy algebra carries a nil radical; the maximal Hausdorff quotient, the small definition — kernel modulo closure of image — through which the whole analytic weight of the theory passes; cohomological parabolic induction in its maximal form, Wong's closed range making Dolbeault cohomology the maximal globalization of its module, the derived functor necessary because plain sections vanish in the ranges that matter; the dominance transfer theorem, Borel-Weil for noncompact groups, vanishing off the compact core's dimension and unitarity transferring from the Levi factor, if and only if under strict dominance; Serre duality for Dolbeault cohomology, the dual of cohomology as compactly supported cohomology of the dual, the hinge that turns the maximal theory into the minimal one; cohomological induction with compact supports, Bratten's theorem in Dolbeault form, the minimal globalization built geometrically, with Borel-Weil recovered at codimension zero; and invariant Hermitian forms as cohomology classes, the destination — forms read as maps, maps as kernels, kernels as classes on the product with the opposite manifold, the invariant forms the diagonal-invariant real classes, and a unitary representation built by completing one at Corollary 9.20. No held concept was revised: three candidates were weighed against the Paper 080 revision's ground and held — the smooth completion states Paper 036's construction truly, the completion of its module as that paper defines and spends it, where this work's smooth globalization is the same theory from its own side, filed as sequence space and as functions on K; and the Casselman-Wallach representation and the Harish-Chandra module scrolls state their own paper's objects, the general theories filed here rather than read back into the scrolls that spend them. No foundation was laid: the theory of completions is filed inside the concepts that use it, the cluster's own ground. Five stated state machines were drawn out — the globalization chain, compact-picture induction, maximal cohomological induction, the minimal dual machine, and the Hermitian-form machine — carrying the held count to 3