Entry 96 — Paper 103 — Breaking the Square-Root Barrier for the Log-Rank Conjecture — Cogentic (no human byline) — a Will-directed test-filing, no queue rank: Will chose this paper from the Cogentic directory assessment in the MOAT chat on 2026-11-25, directing it to be filed end to end with the filing itself as the grade, so the leg ran in the pre-processed shape with no blind seal and the queue untouched. The text stands held entire from the GitHub tree at commit 4a7bc8ab0bc7 — paper.pdf, 823,350 bytes, thirty-seven pages, fetched once and reused for every leg, with the directory README's plain-TeX restatement of the theorem agreeing with the paper's Theorem 1.1 entire. The work itself: a claimed proof that deterministic communication complexity is at most C′ r^{1/2−ε} for every Boolean matrix of real rank r, with ε = 5×10^{−6} and C′ = 750·2^{38} printed in the theorem — past the square-root line that Lovett's γ2 rounding set in 2014 and Sudakov and Tomon's discrepancy argument sharpened in 2023, toward the Lovász–Saks conjecture of 1988 that the true bound is polylogarithmic in r. The proof's architecture, in its own terms: degree-1 methods cannot do better, because a linear tilt's signal is linear in its amplitude while its Kullback–Leibler cost is quadratic, and the biased Rademacher inner-product matrix meets every degree-1 hypothesis while staying KL-stable at the square-root scale — so the only hypothesis left to spend is pointwise Booleanity, S² = 1. Section 3.1 reduces the theorem to the nonexistence of biased KL-stable sign matrices (Theorem 3.1): bias any product measure, minimize a potential, and read off Bregman regularity, sub-Gaussian marginals and one-sided KL stability as the equilibrium's first-order conditions, with Gaussian hyperplane rounding guarding the bias interval's ends. The Boolean identity, projected against the singular vectors, then expresses each linear singular coordinate as a quadratic form governed by third-moment matrices — the cubic identity — and the proof closes by collision: Section 4 strips the bulk's heavy directions with an Efron–Stein bound and Bernoulli shell couplings, Section 5 extracts a variance-weighted map whose hollow quadratic slices carry mean-square energy at least r^{−O(ε)}, and Section 6 annihilates that same energy to r^{−3400ε} with a degree-2 Gaussian tilt whose signal and cost both scale as λ², so the amplitude cancels and the linear cap is escaped.

